AB  - A comprehensive physical understanding of the fouling mechanisms and their influencing factors is essential to overcome their limitations in membrane filtration. This study investigates the impact of particle shape on the fouling behavior at a pore scale. Using a coupled Computational Fluid Dynamics-Discrete Element Method (CFD-DEM) approach, the motion and deposition behavior of non-spherical particles within a membrane-mimicking geometry is simulated under crossflow operation. The findings reveal that non-spherical cakes show the combination of lower coordination numbers (fewer contacts per particle)—which reduces network stability and promotes rearrangement under load—and larger contact areas that enhance stress transmission. Together these features result in more compact and uniform filter cakes, with increased pore intrusion due to stress-driven near-surface rearrangements, compared to spherical particles. Copyright © 2025. Published by Elsevier B.V.
