AB  - Electrochemical pathways that convert CO2 into multi-carbon (C2+) products could mitigate greenhouse gas emissions while supplying chemical building blocks. Realizing this potential requires scalable process designs and optimized operating strategies, including dynamic modes such as pulsed current. Here, we systematically investigate the effects of current density, electrolyte temperature, CO2 excess rate, and pulsed operation on C2+ production in a commercially available flow cell equipped with gas diffusion electrodes (GDEs) and industrially relevant materials. High current densities up to 400 mA cm−2 significantly increased the Faradaic efficiency toward C2+ products. Lower electrolyte temperature (20 °C) enhanced C2+ production compared to 40 °C, likely due to greater CO2 solubility and CO adsorption, which promote CO dimerization. A reduced CO2 excess rate (λ = 2) further favored C2+ formation, potentially by decreasing CO2 adsorption in favor of CO surface coverage. Contrary to prior reports in H-type cells, pulsed current operation did not improve C2+ production in the GDE-equipped flow cell, suggesting that enhanced mass transport minimizes pulsing benefits observed in diffusion-limited systems. This study identifies low temperature, reduced CO2 excess, and statically applied high current densities as key parameters for enhancing C2+ formation under scalable conditions. © 2026 The Author(s). ChemElectroChem published by Wiley-VCH GmbH.
