AB  - This chapter presents selected applications of magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) in filtration processes. It shows the strengths of MRI for studying membrane processes. The overall performance of membrane processes is easily monitored by measuring volume flows or pressures of feed, retentate, and permeate streams. For flow velocity imaging, distilled water was used, and the concentration polarization experiments utilized an oil–water emulsion. The chapter summarizes how MRI measurements were implemented in the field of membrane filtration processes and revealed its possibilities. It discusses in situ monitoring of fouling and cleaning processes with sodium alginate by MRI. The techniques are adaptable to polymeric multichannel membranes. Multichannel membranes consist of several lumen channels embedded in one porous support structure, which reduces fiber breakage and increases the active surface-to-volume ratio. Desalination in membrane filtration is an essential process for drinking water production. © 2022 Wiley-VCH GmbH, Boschstraße 12, 69469 Weinheim, Germany. All rights reserved.
