EP  - 5194
DO  - 10.1039/c8ta10871d
UR  - https://www.scopus.com/pages/publications/85062615197?origin=resultslist
AB  - We report the formation of a reversible complex between CO2 and a bound water coordinating alkaline metal cation (Lewis-acidic water) by nuclear magnetic resonance (NMR) analysis for the first time. Such a unique property of water can facilitate CO2 permeation by offering an additional carrier-mediated pathway, which is distinct from the commonly recognized bicarbonate or carbonate ion-mediated CO2 transport. These findings were exploited to develop an advanced polymeric membrane for separating CO2 directly from wet flue gases. A simply prepared poly(ethylene oxide)-based membrane containing potassium salts exhibits selective super-permeable characteristics for CO2 in humid environments; an exceptionally high CO2 permeance of 4650 gas-permeation units (GPU) with an ideal separation factor of 1500 was achieved for CO2/N2, which lies far above the upper bound of the selectivity-permeability trade-off curve, without any significant performance degradation over 6 months. © 2019 The Royal Society of Chemistry.
